WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.868
-My next guest is
a very funny comedian,

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:05.504
one of my favorite comedians,
I think, of all time.

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:07.973
He has a new book
called "Overstated:

00:00:07.973 --> 00:00:10.777
A Coast to Coast Roast
of the 50 States."

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.412
Does he think maybe I'm trying
to butter him up

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:14.447
and that's why he's going
to be in a bad mood?

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:17.049
Because yeah, when I
bring him out, but I meant it.

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.652
His latest special,
"Colin Quinn &amp; Friends:

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:23.856
A Parking Lot Comedy Show,"
is streaming now on HBO Max.

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:26.025
Here is my buddy Colin Quinn.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:28.194
Wow.
-Whoo. Ah, Jimmy.

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:31.030
-Ah, man, I just said some great
things about you, man.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.033
-I know, but you said
"one of my favorites, I think."

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.467
-Yeah, I did.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.103
-Yeah, but you said "I think"
in the middle of that.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:39.872
I don't know why you had to...
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:41.206
No, I didn't say it.

00:00:41.206 --> 00:00:42.909
Did I say "I think"?
No, you're one of my favorites.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:45.845
-Yeah, you said "you're one
of my favorites, I think."

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.213
-No, no, no, no, no.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:49.081
-Yeah, you started to question
yourself in the middle,

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:50.382
but look, it's still good.

00:00:50.382 --> 00:00:52.785
"One of my favorites, I think"
is pretty good.

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:56.155
It's not good as "one of
my favorites, unequivocally,"

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:58.658
but it's still pretty good.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:01.861
Alright, you were one of
the hardest working comedians

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:04.230
there is,
I would say that about you.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.665
And because of what's happening
in the world,

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.700
you were telling me
this is the longest stretch

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.771
that you've gone
without being in a comedy club.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:15.608
-That's true.
-That must be bizarre for you.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.810
But there's a very
specific demographic

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.845
that you blame
the spread of COVID on.

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:21.747
I hate to bring this up.

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:25.017
-Nerds, because here's
why it's nerds.

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:29.222
Before the invention
of the computer, nerds,

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:31.457
they had one thing
they could actually do in life

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:33.993
that would make them popular,
which is medical field.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.661
They would sit,
look in the microscope.

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:38.130
That was their only hope
to be rock stars,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.765
and they would look at slides.

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:41.134
That was the thing.

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:42.735
And so they would figure out
a thing like COVID,

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.937
but it took them
like three weeks.

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:47.673
And because they're all
too busy, like, you know,

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:50.109
involved with movies and music

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:55.281
and apps, we're getting,
like, third-rate nerds

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.449
to be in the medical field.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:59.519
-[ Laughing ]

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.855
Because they're developing
apps now instead of focusing

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:03.289
on what they should be
nerding about,

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:04.557
which is science and medicine.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.527
-Yeah, which is saving our lives
so we can push them

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:11.030
into lockers for another
couple hundred years.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.366
-Even though you can't perform
right now,

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.234
are you still writing a lot?

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:18.304
-Yes, I'm writing on my ph-- I
write on my phone constantly.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:21.141
I got a few joke ideas.
You want to hear any of them?

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.609
-I'd love to hear some.
-Thank you.

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:27.413
Well, first of all,
corona stuff, obviously.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.115
-Okay.
-In movies,

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:31.917
all those Liam Neeson movies,
like "Taken."

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:33.986
-Uh-huh.
-Right?

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:36.255
Now it's going to change, like,
"I will look for you,

00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:37.790
I will find you,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.192
and I will give you
the finger across the room.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:43.062
It will be awkward."

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.830
Sounds like a sketch
that got cut at dress,

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.133
now that I say it out loud.
-Not bad.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.402
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.304
'Cause you can't go near people,
the six feet.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:54.507
I understand.
-What's that? Exactly.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:03:00.746
And sex scenes, when you
explain it, it's even worse.

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:03.949
Sex scenes -- there's going
to be no more sex.

00:03:03.949 --> 00:03:05.818
People are not going to kiss
in a movie or the whole audience

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.320
is going to go like, "Ugh!"

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.889
Imagine two people exchanging
sal-- we're all going to think

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:11.857
like, "What is that person's
husband and wife going to do

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:13.625
with these perverts out there."

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:15.962
It's going to go back
to like the 19-- in the 1940s,

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.596
people would just wink
at each other in a movie

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:18.798
and that was like sex.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:21.501
Or they would, like, have
bunk beds on a train,

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.869
and you were like, "Ooh,"
and wear pajamas.

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:25.504
They all wore pajamas.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:28.908
And that was like, "Ooh my God,
pajamas, that's a hot movie."

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:32.144
-Bunk beds on a train.
Oh, my gosh.

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:34.279
These are great bits.

00:03:34.279 --> 00:03:36.815
-They're the beginnings
of great bits, thank you.

00:03:36.815 --> 00:03:39.118
-I want to talk about the book.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:41.453
Gosh, it's so funny.

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:42.588
It's Called "overstated:

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:46.058
A Coast to Coast Roast
of the 50 States,"

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.861
and nice quotes
in the back here.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.129
A few quotes.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.533
"New York Times" called you
a bar-stool philosopher.

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.434
That's a compliment, right?

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:56.635
-It depends on the bar.

00:03:56.635 --> 00:04:00.406
You know, sometimes it's,
you know, some erudite

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:05.811
Irish intellectual professor
with his corduroy jacket,

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:09.181
spouting Joyce or it could be
a drunken lout who's like,

00:04:09.181 --> 00:04:11.149
"Hey, I'll tell you
what I think is going on."

00:04:11.149 --> 00:04:13.686
You know what I mean? So I don't
know how they meant it.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.089
It sounds like a little bit
of a left-handed compliment.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.191
-Yeah, a little.
-Maybe I'm a little paranoid.

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.893
-I want to talk to you
about this special

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.761
because you figured out a way
to do a socially

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:24.496
distant stand-up show.

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:26.832
You have a new special
on HBO Max.

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:29.469
"Colin Quinn &amp; Friends:
A Parking Lot Comedy Show."

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:31.570
You shot it at a drive-in
theater in Brooklyn.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.840
I didn't even know there was
a drive-in theater in Brooklyn.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.942
-Yeah.
-How did the idea come together?

00:04:36.942 --> 00:04:38.444
How did you do this?
You direct it.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.846
-Well, I did a tweet in March
and then my manager was like,

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:42.114
"We should do that
as a real thing."

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:44.750
I tweeted March 3rd
or something,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.720
"Hey, drive-in comedy,"
trying to be funny on Twitter.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.124
You know how I like to mix it up
a little, and then he goes --

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:54.093
he started pitching it around,
then HBO Max was like,

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.394
"Yeah, we'll do a special."

00:04:55.394 --> 00:05:00.032
So then I get my friends
and it's basically us doing

00:05:00.032 --> 00:05:03.102
a socially distant --
doing stand-up for cars,

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:07.506
which, you know, like 50 cars
in a parking lot,

00:05:07.506 --> 00:05:09.675
and it was funny.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.777
A lot of people,
like the emcee Rich Vos goes,

00:05:11.777 --> 00:05:14.246
"Hey, don't worry, folks.
We don't have bouncers here.

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:16.048
If you give us trouble,
we have tow trucks."

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:17.917
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.685
You know what I liked about it
is also

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:22.755
not just the acts on stage
but also a lot of the --

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:25.591
just what comedians
talk about backstage.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.694
-Yeah.
-And hanging out.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:30.062
-All that banter.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:32.098
All the fun of the -- you know,

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:35.068
that communal bonding
that you miss

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:36.902
because you no longer have that.

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:39.272
Because you're by yourself
in a studio.

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:41.107
You don't even have an audience
anymore.

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:43.509
You're literally back
to being alone.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:45.010
I've seen them online
every night.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:49.549
It's you as a little boy in
Saugerties with a hair brush

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.016
in front of your mirror
doing jokes

00:05:51.016 --> 00:05:53.019
you don't even know if they're
getting laughs or not.

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:56.155
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:58.758
-You get right in there,
you know.

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:02.161
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.197
It is a bit awkward, you know.

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:07.633
Do you remember the most awkward
gig you ever had?

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:10.636
-Well, I mean, compared to,
like, the car gig was --

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.238
that's what I was trying
to tell people.

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:13.839
We've all had so many gigs.

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:15.207
You know, in comedy,

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:17.677
we're suddenly,
like, oh, doing comedy clubs.

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:19.111
That's how it works,
and then suddenly you're next to

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.813
somebody's pool in a backyard,

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:22.047
and you're like, "Wait a minute.

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.317
I'm supposed to do comedy here,
right?"

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:25.551
-That was awful.

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:27.886
-My famous bad gig
is De Niro's birthday party,

00:06:27.886 --> 00:06:29.621
his 60th birthday party,

00:06:29.621 --> 00:06:33.992
and I went in there
and ruined his birthday.

00:06:33.992 --> 00:06:35.828
Here's how bad it was
'cause even I was like,

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:38.030
"Maybe I'm exaggerating.
It wasn't that bad.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.632
He's had such a life
filled with so many things.

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:40.799
He probably doesn't
even remember it."

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:42.668
And then like 10 years later,

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:44.203
Jim Norton -- we happened
to talking to De Niro

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:45.838
outside of some thing,
and Jim Norton goes,

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:47.806
"Remember the time he bombed
at your birthday party?"

00:06:47.806 --> 00:06:49.007
And De Niro and his wife

00:06:49.007 --> 00:06:52.144
both got this look
in their face of hate.

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:54.180
It was that -- 10 years later.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.781
-Wow!
-The guy's traveled the world

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.382
eight times since then probably,

00:06:57.382 --> 00:07:00.086
and it still brought
an ugly energy to both of them.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:02.321
That's how bad it was.
-What was so bad about it?

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:04.490
-I just went up there,
and I was planning on --

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.492
you know, I had this big plan.

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:07.993
She just told me to go up
and do an impression

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:09.261
of De Niro and leave.

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:10.663
But I was like Ralph Kramden.

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:12.531
"No, I'll do more than that."

00:07:12.531 --> 00:07:13.932
And she's like, "Alright.

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:15.401
I mean, he knows what
he's doing. He's a comedian."

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:17.235
-"We're not asking for more."
-I don't know what I'm doing.

00:07:17.235 --> 00:07:19.371
Yeah. I was roasting him.

00:07:19.371 --> 00:07:22.374
The crowd didn't know --
I told her, "Don't intro me,"

00:07:22.374 --> 00:07:24.410
which is another
amateur mistake.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.611
Nobody knows who I am.
-No introduction.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:28.214
-I go up
and I start trashing him,

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:29.648
making roast jokes,
and people are like --

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:31.350
They're in the middle of dinner,
Jimmy.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.985
It's not even dark out.
It's light out.

00:07:33.985 --> 00:07:35.954
So everybody's there.

00:07:35.954 --> 00:07:40.292
So Robin Williams -- I leave
soaked in shame at the end.

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:42.494
I'm walking outside,
but it was slightly raining,

00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:44.863
and I had this shirt, like, this
new cashmere shirt on a hanger.

00:07:44.863 --> 00:07:46.065
I had another show that night,

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.401
so I had a shirt,
and Robin Williams comes out.

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:49.735
He's, like, commiserating
with me,

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:51.370
and I go, "I just don't know."

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.672
And I gesture, and my shirt
flies off the hanger

00:07:53.672 --> 00:07:56.275
into a puddle and a cab runs it
over in the rain.

00:07:56.275 --> 00:07:58.643
Robin Williams
couldn't stop laughing.

00:07:58.643 --> 00:08:00.145
I just walked away.
I didn't even say another word.

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:02.581
I just left.
I saw him like two years later.

00:08:02.581 --> 00:08:03.782
-That's the best.

00:08:03.782 --> 00:08:06.018
Talk about the worst gig.
It just got worse.

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:08.020
Oh, my God. That's perfect.
-It was brutal.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.756
-But I love that he's there
to laugh. I love that comic.

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.791
You need a comedian.
-Thank God he was -- Yes.

00:08:12.791 --> 00:08:13.993
He understood.

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:15.795
In fact, he said his wife
was going, "Help him."

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:18.263
He goes, "No, he doesn't want me
to help him."

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:20.966
-Well, I want to show
a clip of your special.

00:08:20.966 --> 00:08:25.003
This is "Colin Quinn &amp; Friends:
A Parking Lot Comedy Show."

00:08:25.003 --> 00:08:27.706
Take a look at this clip.

00:08:27.706 --> 00:08:30.676
-You've never done a show
with all cars, right?

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:32.311
-Not really, no.

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:34.279
-I'm just wondering
how it's going to work.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:36.916
-Oh, yeah, boss, we got to talk
to you about the instructions.

00:08:36.916 --> 00:08:40.552
So when you come out, a guy is
gonna change the microphone.

00:08:40.552 --> 00:08:43.555
He might be changing it while
you're on stage doing the act.

00:08:43.555 --> 00:08:45.625
-What the [bleep]?
What are you talking about?

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:49.262
-The guy's gonna be on stage.
-As I'm talking?

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:51.497
Safety first, they say,
with this COVID.

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:53.065
Thank you for cleaning this off.

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:54.666
Listen, you're a big name.

00:08:54.666 --> 00:08:57.335 line:0%
And after this, believe me,
you're going to be able

00:08:57.335 --> 00:08:58.671 line:0%
to do rooftops.

00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:01.807
You're going to be able to do
[bleep] driveways.

00:09:01.807 --> 00:09:04.376
Do you know how many --
Parking garages.

00:09:04.376 --> 00:09:05.877
It's not going to end for you.

00:09:05.877 --> 00:09:07.446
-We got a big treehouse
show coming up.

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:08.914
-Yes.

00:09:08.914 --> 00:09:10.349
-Colin Quinn, everybody.

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:13.853
His book "Overstated: A Coast
to Coast Roast of the 50 States"

00:09:13.853 --> 00:09:16.722
is available now and check out
"Colin Quinn &amp; Friends:

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:19.525
A Parking Lot Comedy Show"
on HBO Max.

00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:22.561
It is so fun to have you
and always a delight,

00:09:22.561 --> 00:09:25.531
and please, come back whenever,
buddy, and stay safe, okay?

00:09:25.531 --> 00:09:27.033
-Thanks, Jimmy, you too.

